'''Everton Blair Jr.''' (born April 23, 1992)<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date= |title=US House of Representatives Archives - Everton Blair Jr. Biography |url=https://history.house.gov/People/Listing/B/BLAIR,-Everton,-Jr--(B001328)/}}</ref> is an American politician and educator serving as the [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] for {{ushr|GA|13}} since September 2026. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], he won a [[2026 Georgia's 13th congressional district special election|special election]] to fill the unexpired term of the late [[David Scott (Georgia politician)|David Scott]]. Blair is the first openly [[African-American LGBTQ community|LGBT African American]] elected to represent [[Southern United States|a Southern state]] in Congress.

Blair previously served as a member and chair of the [[Gwinnett County School District|Gwinnet County School Board]]. He ran in the [[2026 United States House of Representatives elections in Georgia#District 13|regularly-scheduled primary election]] for a full term in May 2026, placing third with 11.6% of the vote.<ref name="hg">{{Cite web |last=|date=19 May 2026|title=Jasmine Clark wins Democratic nomination for U.S. House in Georgia's 13th Congressional District|url=https://www.kare11.com/article/syndication/associatedpress/jasmine-clark-wins-democratic-nomination-for-us-house-in-georgias-13th-congressional-district/616-2667734c-a097-4d86-b3d7-11b551abfe9f|access-date=2026-08-26|website=[[Kare11]]|language=en|agency=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Blair was born in [[Gwinnett County, Georgia]] in 1992 to [[computer scientist]] Everton Blair Sr. originally of [[Kingston, Jamaica]], and British born [[Jamaican-American]] [[Emory University]] medical student Fiona Anderson, who became a [[pediatrician]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=https://www.abcpediatricgroup.com/dr-fiona-blair-pediatrician.php |title=Fiona Blair, MD |url=https://www.abcpediatricgroup.com/dr-fiona-blair-pediatrician.php |access-date=2026-08-29 |website=ABC Pediatric Group {{!}} Stone Mountain Pediatricians |language=en}}</ref> The family moved from [[DeKalb County, Georgia|DeKalb County]] to Gwinnett in 1996 and Blair graduated from [[Shiloh High School (Georgia)|Shiloh High School]] in 2009.<ref name="Edweek">{{cite web|url=https://www.edweek.org/leadership/how-the-fight-for-americas-suburbs-started-in-public-schools/2020/10|title=How the Fight for America's Suburbs Started in Public Schools|first=Benjamin|last=Herold|date=October 26, 2020|access-date=August 25, 2026|website=[[Edweek]]}}</ref>

He then graduated from [[Harvard College]] with a bachelor's degree in applied mathematics and from [[Stanford University]] with a master's degree in policy, organizations and leadership. He returned to the [[Harvard Graduate School of Education]] as a Presidential Public Service Fellow in 2023 and earned a Doctor of Educational Leadership.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://service.harvard.edu/people/everton-blair-jr|title=Everton Blair, Jr.|access-date=August 25, 2026|website=[[Harvard University]]}}</ref>

==Career==
Following graduation from Stanford, Blair participated in the White House Initiative on Educational Excellence for African Americans fellowship in the [[Obama administration]] then took a job at [[Los Angeles, California|Los Angeles]]-based [[superintendent (education)|superintendent]]-training program Broad Academy.<ref name="Edweek"/> He taught in [[Atlanta]] for two years through [[Teach for America]].<ref name="WABE"/>

In 2018, Blair was elected to the District 4 seat of the [[Gwinnett County School District|Gwinnett County School Board]], defeating longtime [[GOP|Republican]] incumbent Chuck Studebaker and becoming the first person of color on the board.<ref name="WABE">{{cite web|url=https://www.wabe.org/gwinnett-school-board-gets-its-first-member-of-color/|title=Gwinnett School Board Gets Its First Member Of Color|first=Martha|last=Dalton|date=November 7, 2018|access-date=August 25, 2026|website=[[WABE-TV]]}}</ref> He filed to run for the [[Georgia State Superintendent of Schools]] in [[2022 Georgia State Superintendent of Schools election|2022]], but ultimately withdrew due to personal reasons in March 2022 before the primary election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.gwinnettdailypost.com/local/gwinnett-school-board-member-everton-blair-suspends-campaign-to-be-georgias-state-school-superintendent/article_d69ed276-9c3e-11ec-9d02-efaf0e9738d4.html|title=Everton Blair suspends campaign to be Georgia's state school superintendent|first=Curt|last=Yeomans|date=March 5, 2022|access-date=August 25, 2026|website=[[Gwinnett Daily Post]]}}</ref> His term on the Gwinnett County school board ended in January 2023.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.ajc.com/education/everton-blair-exits-gwinnett-school-board-after-historic-four-years/5CQCVJ72WNFBVASS23UGPU2UXA/|title=Everton Blair exits Gwinnett school board after historic four years|first=Josh|last=Reyes|date=January 19, 2023|access-date=August 25, 2026|website=[[The Atlanta Journal-Constitution]]}}</ref>

=== 2026 U.S. House campaigns ===
{{Main|2026 United States House of Representatives elections in Georgia#District 13|2026 Georgia's 13th congressional district special election}}
In April 2025, Blair announced his candidacy for the [[United States House of Representatives]] election in [[Georgia's 13th congressional district]] in [[2026 United States House of Representatives elections in Georgia#District 13|2026]], challenging incumbent Democrat [[David Scott (Georgia politician)|David Scott]], who was seeking a 13th term.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bluestein |first=Greg |date=April 16, 2025 |title=Former Gwinnett school board chair jumps in race to challenge 12-term Rep. David Scott |url=https://www.ajc.com/politics/democrat-everton-blair-jumps-in-race-to-challenge-rep-david-scott/25GGX5HTRVFVXPHD73ZIQPADHY/ |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=ajc |language=en}}</ref> Scott had been in poor health,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hill |first=Meredith Lee |last2=Downs |first2=Garrett |date=February 21, 2024 |title='Exhibit A for term limits': Some Democrats question Rep. David Scott's reelection bid |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2024/02/20/david-scott-reelection-questioned-democrats-00142290 |access-date=August 26, 2026 |website=Politico}}</ref> and Blair was one of a number of candidates to challenge him in the Democratic primary.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitchell |first=Tia |date=August 28, 2025 |title=Congressman David Scott faces a new wave of Georgia challengers |url=https://www.ajc.com/politics/2025/08/congressman-david-scott-faces-a-new-wave-of-georgia-challengers |access-date=August 26, 2026 |website=The Atlanta Journal-Constitution}}</ref> Scott died on April 22, 2026, though his name remained on the Democratic primary ballot.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mitchell |first1=Tia |date=April 22, 2026 |title=Georgia U.S. Rep. David Scott has died |url=https://www.ajc.com/politics/2026/04/georgia-us-rep-david-scott-has-died/ |access-date=August 26, 2026 |website=The Atlanta Journal-Constitution}}</ref> Blair subsequently announced his candidacy for the [[2026 Georgia's 13th congressional district special election|special election]] to fill the remainder of Scott's term to the [[119th United States Congress|119th Congress]], to be held after the regular Democratic primary for the [[120th United States Congress|120th Congress]].<ref name="cq">{{Cite web |last=Darnell |first=Tim |date=2026-05-14 |title=6 candidates qualify in special election to replace late Rep. David Scott in Congress |url=https://www.atlantanewsfirst.com/2026/05/14/6-candidates-qualify-special-election-replace-late-rep-david-scott-congress/ |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=AtlantaNewsFirst.com |language=en}}</ref>

On June 23, 2026, Blair was defeated in the regular Democratic primary, finishing third behind Heavenly Kimes and  eventual winner, state Representative [[Jasmine Clark]], with 11.6% of the vote.<ref name="hg"/> Despite his defeat in the regular election primary, Blair continued his campaign for the special election.

In the special election, Blair's main opponent was Marcye Scott, David Scott's daughter and a fellow Democrat.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Davis |first1=Sarah |date=April 30, 2026 |title=David Scott's daughter to run to fill his Georgia House seat: Report |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/5857395-david-scott-daughter-house-special-election/ |access-date=August 26, 2026 |website=The Hill}}</ref> Clark was not a candidate in the special election.<ref name="cq" /> Scott and Blair advanced to a runoff after no candidate received 50% of the vote in the first round, held on July 28. Scott received 46.0% of the vote to Blair's 37.4%.<ref name="GaSOS2026SpecialPDF">{{cite web |date=July 2026 |title=Official Results of the July 28, 2026 Special Election |url=https://ga.gov |publisher=[[Georgia Secretary of State]] |format=PDF |access-date=August 6, 2026}}</ref> In the runoff, held on August 25, Blair defeated Scott 53.9% to 46.1%, securing a seat in Congress for the remainder of David Scott's term.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kallis |first=Sarah |date=2026-08-26 |title=Everton Blair wins 13th District special election runoff {{!}} Georgia Public Broadcasting |url=https://www.gpb.org/news/2026/08/26/everton-blair-wins-13th-district-special-election-runoff |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=www.gpb.org |language=en}}</ref> Blair's victory made him the first openly [[LGBTQ people in the United States|LGBTQ person]] elected to Congress from Georgia, and the first openly gay black person elected to Congress from any Southern state.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.advocate.com/politics/election/everton-blair-wins-georgia|title=Gay Georgia Democrat Everton Blair wins U.S. House seat &#124; Advocate.com|website=www.advocate.com}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Blair [[came out]] as [[gay]] on [[National Coming Out Day]] in October 2019.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/feature/nbc-out/georgia-school-official-comes-out-gay-nationalcomingoutday-n1065001|title=Georgia school official comes out as gay on #NationalComingOutDay|date=October 11, 2019|access-date=August 25, 2026|website=[[NBC News]]|first=Tim|last=Fitzsimmons}}</ref>

