'''Caroline Moquin-Joubert''' (born 1999) is a Canadian [[flag football]] player. She plays as a [[wide receiver]] and is a member of the [[Canada women's national flag football team|Canada national team]]. She also played her [[college ice hockey]] career with [[Concordia Stingers women's ice hockey|Concordia University]] in Montréal.

==College career==
Moquin-Joubert played for the [[Concordia Stingers]]' women's flag football and [[Concordia Stingers women's ice hockey|ice hockey team]] for four seasons, the latter with whom she helped them win the [[U Sports women's ice hockey championship|U Sports national championship]] in 2022 and 2024.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://stingers.ca/news.php?id=2115 |title=Women's hockey crowned RSEQ champions for third year in a row |publisher=[[Concordia Stingers]] |date=March 6, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://stingers.ca/news.php?id=2120 |title=Women's hockey cap off historic season with National Title |publisher=[[Concordia Stingers]] |date=March 18, 2024}}</ref>

==Flag football career==
Moquin-Joubert competed in the [[2024 IFAF Women's Flag Football World Championship]], where she contributed to the team finishing in fifth place and qualify for the [[2025 World Games]].<ref name="2024 Roster">{{cite web |title=Senior Women's Flag National Team |url=https://footballcanada.com/event/2024-ifaf-world-flag-football-championships/ |publisher=Football Canada |access-date=28 December 2024}}</ref><ref name="2024 IFAF">{{cite web |last1=Stachel |first1=Braydon |title=Canadian Women's Team secures 5th place and World Games berth at 2024 IFAF World Flag Championship |url=https://footballcanada.com/news/canadian-womens-team-secures-5th-place-and-world-games-berth-at-2024-ifaf-world-flag-championship/ |publisher=Football Canada |access-date=28 December 2024 |date=5 Sep 2024}}</ref> At the [[Flag football at the 2025 World Games|2025 World Games]], she was part of the Canada squad that won the bronze after defeating [[Austria women's national flag football team|Austria]] in the score of 38-20.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://footballcanada.com/news/football-canada-announces-senior-womens-flag-roster-ahead-of-2025-world-games-in-chengdu-china/|title=Football Canada Announces Senior Women’s Flag Roster ahead of 2025 World Games in Chengdu, China|publisher=[[Football Canada]]|date=July 17, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.olympics.com/en/news/the-world-games-2025-flag-football-mexico-usa-historic-title-defence|title=The World Games 2025 Flag Football: Mexico fend off USA in historic title defence|publisher=Olympics.com|date=August 17, 2025}}</ref> The following month, she took part in [[2025 IFAF Americas Continental Women's Flag Football Championship]], where her team won the silver medal.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://footballcanada.com/news/preview-senior-womens-national-flag-team-set-to-compete-at-the-2025-ifaf-americas-continental-championship-in-panama-on-september-12-14/|title=PREVIEW: Senior Women’s National Flag team set to compete at the 2025 IFAF Americas Continental Championship in Panama on September 12–14|publisher=[[Football Canada]]|date=September 8, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://3downnation.com/2025/09/14/team-canada-wins-silver-bronze-at-ifaf-americas-flag-football-continental-championship/|title=Team Canada earns silver, bronze at IFAF Americas Flag Football Continental Championship|publisher=3DownNation|date=September 14, 2025}}</ref>

In August 2026, Moquin-Joubert contributed to the Canadian squad that won the gold medal in the [[2026 IFAF Women's Flag Football World Championship]], enabling the team to qualify the [[Flag football at the 2028 Summer Olympics|2028 Summer Olympics]] in [[Los Angeles]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://footballcanada.com/news/football-canada-announces-senior-womens-and-mens-national-flag-football-team-for-2026-ifaf-flag-football-world-championships/|title=Football Canada Announces Senior Women’s and Men’s National Flag Football Team for 2026 IFAF Flag Football World Championships|publisher=[[Football Canada]]|date=July 17, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Canada women clinch berth in LA 2028 with semifinal win at flag football world championships|url=https://www.cbc.ca/sports/olympics/canada-flag-football-world-championship-august-15-9.7308642|website=[[CBC Sports]]|publisher=[[Canadian Broadcasting Corporation]] (CBC)|location=Toronto, Ontario, Canada|date=August 15, 2026|access-date=August 15, 2026}}</ref>

