'''Jontee Brown''' (born 24 December 1997) is a [[wheelchair basketball]] player from Australia.<ref name="ba">{{cite web |title=Jontee Brown |url=https://www.australia.basketball/players/4040979 |website=Basketball Australia website |accessdate=17 September 2018}}</ref>

==Early life==
Jontee Brown was born in [[Canberra]] on 24 December 1997 and rased in [[Bendigo]].<ref name=":0">{{cite web|url=https://rollt-magazin.de/interview-with-jontee-brown-i-really-want-to-improve-my-game-and-become-a-more-consistent-and-dominant-scorer/|title=Interview with Jontee Brown: "I really want to improve my game and become a more consistent and dominant scorer"|publisher=Rollt.Magazin|date=11 September 2019}}</ref> After falling into a sinkhole in 2015, he was diagnosed with reactive arthritis in his left knee as well as ankylosing spondylitis in his lower spine.<ref name=":0"/><ref name=":1">{{cite web |title=Bendigo 2018 Young Citizen of the Year |url=https://www.latrobe.edu.au/news/articles/2018/release/bendigo-2018-young-citizen-of-the-year |website=La Trobe University |accessdate=17 September 2026}}</ref>

==Career==
Brown started playing wheelchair basketball in May 2016.<ref name=":0"/><ref name=":1"/> In 2016, he began playing with the Bendigo Braves on the local level and the Kilsyth Cobras of the [[National Wheelchair Basketball League (Australia)|NWBL]] the following year.<ref name=":0"/> He also played for the German team Köln 99ers and the French team CS Meaux.<ref name=":0"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bendigoadvertiser.com.au/story/5331837/jontee-brown-earns-overseas-basketball-contract/|title=Jontee Brown earns overseas basketball contract|publisher=Bendigo Advertiser|date=9 April 2018}}</ref> In 2025, he was signed by the Wollongong Roller Hawks of the NWBL and later [[Beşiktaş JK (wheelchair basketball)|Beşiktaş]] of the [[Turkish Wheelchair Basketball Super League]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://rollerhawks.com.au/News/jontee-brown-signs-with-wollongong.html|title=Jontee Brown Signs with Wollongong|publisher=Wollongong Roller Hawks|access-date=17 September 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.illawarramercury.com.au/story/9301737/jontee-brown-rejoins-wollongong-roller-hawks-undefeated-squad-what-it-means/|title='He'll make a huge difference': star set for comeback after Turkey stint|publisher=Illawarra Mercury|date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

At the 2025 Asia-Oceania Championships, Brown was a member of the Rollers that won the championship after defeating Japan 62–57.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.basketballact.com.au/news/jontee-brown-heading-to-europe-with-australian-rollers/|title=Jontee Brown heading to Europe with Australian Rollers|publisher=Basketball ACT|date=14 August 2025|access-date=17 September 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=15 November 2025 |title=Rollers Strike Gold At Asia-Oceania Championships |url=https://www.paralympic.org.au/2025/11/rollers-strike-gold/ |access-date=17 September 2026 |website=Basketball Australia |language=en-AU}}</ref> In September 2026, he was part of the national squad that won the [[2026 Wheelchair Basketball World Championships]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.australia.basketball/news/4554214/rollers-announce-world-championship-team|title=Rollers announce World Championship team|publisher=Basketball Australia|date=4 August 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.australia.basketball/news/4578944/rollers-crowned-world-champions-dominates-italy-to-claim-gold|title=Rollers crowned World Champions, dominates Italy to claim Gold|publisher=Basketball Australia|date=19 September 2026}}</ref>

Brown is a [[Victorian Institute of Sport]] scholarship athlete.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Jontee Brown |url=https://vis.org.au/athletes/jontee-brown |access-date=2026-09-16 |website=Victorian Institute of Sport |language=en}}</ref>

